19. Apply this to a backend system design interview
Suppose an interviewer asks you to design an internal company-document chatbot. Your explanation should connect the concepts to concrete engineering decisions.
Interview concern	What you should discuss
Document ingestion	Extract text, chunk it, generate embeddings, and index it
Query handling	Authenticate the user, embed the question, and retrieve relevant chunks
Data security	Enforce document-level authorization during retrieval
LLM integration	Construct prompts, call the API, handle timeouts and retries appropriately
Cost and latency	Limit retrieved context, manage token budgets, and measure usage
Response quality	Evaluate retrieval, validate output, cite sources, and abstain when evidence is insufficient
Document updates	Re-index changed content and remove obsolete vectors
Reliability	Handle model failures, empty retrieval results, rate limits, and malformed outputs
One important production distinction: the LLM should not be your authorization system or your source of truth. Your backend must enforce access controls and verify important business facts independently.
Final takeaway
You should be able to explain this chain without hesitation:
Tokens represent text → embeddings represent items numerically → vector databases retrieve similar items → RAG supplies external context → Transformers use attention to process context → trained parameters determine model behavior → inference generates the response.
Pre-training creates the initial model, fine-tuning can adapt it, and temperature influences generation variability. None of these guarantees factual correctness.